<header><p class="st-kicker">tribal confederation · Italy</p><h1 class="st-title">Lucanians</h1><p class="st-meta">420 BCE – 272 BCE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Oscan-speaking people who took over much of southern Italy in the 5th and 4th centuries BCE, conquering Greek Poseidonia and defeating Thurii.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Lucanians formed in the 5th century BCE from Samnite groups moving south into the mountains of modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basilicata">Basilicata</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cilento">Cilento</a>. They absorbed or displaced earlier peoples, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oenotrians">Oenotrians</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chones">Chones</a>, and pressed the Greek cities of the coast, capturing <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/poseidonia">Poseidonia</a> around 410 BCE.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>They lived in fortified hill settlements such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serra-di-vaglio">Serra di Vaglio</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roccagloriosa">Roccagloriosa</a>, and were led in war by elected kings. Their federal sanctuary of the goddess <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mefitis">Mefitis</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rossano-di-vaglio">Rossano di Vaglio</a> has yielded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oscan-language">Oscan</a> inscriptions in Greek letters. Painted tombs at <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/poseidonia">Paestum</a> show armoured horsemen and funeral games. They defeated <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/thurii">Thurii</a> near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laus">Laus</a> in 389 BCE and killed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-i-of-epirus">Alexander of Epirus</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-pandosia">Pandosia</a> in 331 BCE.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/bruttii">Bruttii</a> broke away from them in 356 BCE. The Lucanians allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in 298 BCE, then turned against it with Pyrrhus and were subdued by 272 BCE. They later backed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hannibal">Hannibal</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/social-war-91-87-bce">Social War</a> rebels, and their region became the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lucania">Roman Lucania</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/lucanians.jpg" alt="Lucanians" width="320" height="288"><figcaption>Image: Ilario · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Osco_diffusione.gif" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>420 BCE – 272 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/5th-century-bce">5th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>tribal confederation</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Oscan polytheism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Oscan</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Lucani, Lucanian League</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-346&amp;focus=lucanians" aria-label="Lucanians on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4950 614 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Lucanians lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5304 942h0M5336 935h0M5402 1025h0M5368 989h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5270 870h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M5270 870h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Italy. <a href="/atlas?year=-346&amp;focus=lucanians">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

